<article class="env-lemma" id="03GI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03GI">Lemma <span data-tag="03GI">26.21.14</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be morphisms of schemes. If $g \circ f$ is quasi-compact and $g$ is quasi-separated then $f$ is quasi-compact. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because $f$ equals the composition $(1, f) : X \to X \times _ Z Y \to Y$. The first map is quasi-compact by Lemma <a href="/tag/01KT" data-tag="01KT">26.21.11</a> because it is a section of the quasi-separated morphism $X \times _ Z Y \to X$ (a base change of $g$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01KU" data-tag="01KU">26.21.12</a>). The second map is quasi-compact as it is the base change of $g \circ f$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01K5" data-tag="01K5">26.19.3</a>. And compositions of quasi-compact morphisms are quasi-compact, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01K6" data-tag="01K6">26.19.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>